        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 7,
        "titulo": "Haben and the direct object",
        "explicacao": "«Haben» (to have) is irregular. The direct object of «haben» goes into the accusative. In the masculine singular, «ein» becomes «einen» and «kein» becomes «keinen»; in the feminine, the neuter and the plural nothing changes: «Ich habe einen Bruder. Ich habe eine Schwester. Ich habe ein Kind. Ich habe keine Kinder».",
        "comparacao": "In English the direct object does not change form (apart from pronouns: «he» becomes «him»). In German the masculine changes: «ein Bruder» (who someone is) becomes «einen Bruder» (what someone has).",
